"""
NCERT Class 5 Mathematics - Chapter 3: Angles as Turns Data
"""

slides_data = [{'id': 1, 'heading': 'Turning Around', 'tagline': 'Movement, Rotation, and the Spark of Geometry', 'color': 'from-amber-500 to-orange-600', 'badge': 'Class 5 Maths • Chapter 3', 'audio_text': 'Welcome, young mathematicians! Today we step into an exciting world where every physical turn creates a mathematical angle. Think of a spinning giant wheel at an amusement park, a turning doorknob, or standing in the playground and pivoting on your heels to face your friend. When an object or person rotates around a fixed point, it is not moving straight forward; rather, it is executing a turn. In mathematics, we measure this circular movement to understand direction, orientation, and shape. Whenever an arm or ray pivots around a stationary centre, it sweeps out a portion of circular space. Notice how the blades of a ceiling fan continuously rotate around the central motor, or how the hands of a clock journey smoothly across the numbered dial. All geometric shapes, from triangular road signs to rectangular book covers, are formed by lines that meet after turning by precise amounts. By observing turns in our daily life, we uncover the very foundation of geometry. Let us explore how rotation turns everyday action into mathematical wonder!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'What Is a Turn?', 'content': 'A turn is a circular rotational movement around a stationary fixed point called the centre or pivot. Rather than travelling along a straight line, an object changes its heading or facing direction by rotating.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Everyday Turns', 'content': 'We encounter turns everywhere: giant wheel cabins sweeping through the sky, clock hands ticking around the dial, revolving doors, bicycle pedals, and gymnasts spinning on the mat.', 'icon': '🎡'}, {'title': 'The Geometric Connection', 'content': 'Geometry is not just frozen lines on paper. Angles are dynamic measurements of how much one line has rotated away from another line from a shared starting position.', 'icon': '📐'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What kind of motion takes place when a giant wheel rotates around its central axle?', 'options': ['Rotational turning motion around a fixed pivot', 'Straight-line linear motion across the ground', 'Random zigzag drifting without a fixed centre', 'Periodic vertical bounce without angle change'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! A giant wheel rotates around its fixed central axle, sweeping out turns that form geometric angles.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following is the best real-world example of turning movement?', 'options': ['A minute hand sweeping around a clock dial', 'A train moving along a perfectly straight track', 'An apple falling straight down from a tree branch', 'A ruler lying flat and stationary on a desk'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! The minute hand continuously pivots around the dial centre, illustrating rotational turning.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'heading': 'Turn Types', 'tagline': 'Full Turns, Half Turns, and Quarter Turns', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Core Fractions of Rotation', 'audio_text': 'Now let us classify turns by looking at how much circular distance has been completed. Imagine standing in the schoolyard facing North. If you turn all the way around until you face North once again, you have executed one full turn. A full turn completes a whole circle of rotation. What if you turn halfway around so that you now face directly South, the opposite direction? That is a half turn, representing exactly 1/2 of a full rotation. If you start facing North and turn just enough to face East, you have made a quarter turn, which is exactly 1/4 of a full circle. If you make two quarter turns in the same direction, 1/4 plus 1/4 equals 2/4, which simplifies to 1/2, a half turn! And if you make four quarter turns, you complete 4/4, which brings you right back to your starting direction as a complete whole turn. Straw models, clock hands, and compass pointers help us clearly observe and verify these fundamental rotational fractions.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '1 Full Turn (Whole Circle)', 'content': 'Rotating completely around a pivot back to the exact initial facing direction. Represented as 1 whole turn or 4 quarter turns.', 'icon': '⭕'}, {'title': '1/2 Turn (Half Turn)', 'content': 'Rotating halfway across the circle, facing the exact opposite direction. Exactly equal to 2 quarter turns (1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2).', 'icon': '↔️'}, {'title': '1/4 Turn (Quarter Turn)', 'content': 'Rotating one-fourth of a circle. Transforms facing direction from North to East, or East to South, producing a classic square corner.', 'icon': '↗️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many quarter (1/4) turns are required to complete one full turn?', 'options': ['4 quarter turns', '2 quarter turns', '3 quarter turns', '8 quarter turns'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Exactly! 4 quarter turns combine to make 4/4 = 1 complete whole turn.'}, {'question': 'If you are facing North and perform a 1/2 turn, which direction will you face?', 'options': ['South', 'East', 'West', 'North'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! A 1/2 turn rotates you halfway around the circle to face the exact opposite direction, South.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'heading': 'What Is Angle', 'tagline': 'The Amount of Turning Between Two Arms at a Vertex', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Geometric Definition', 'audio_text': 'We are now ready to formalize what an angle truly is in mathematics. Take two straight rays or arms connected together at a single common endpoint. This shared meeting point is known as the vertex. When both arms lie directly on top of each other, there is zero opening. As we keep one arm fixed and rotate the second arm outward around the vertex, a gap or opening is created between them. An angle is defined as the measure of the amount of turning required to rotate one arm from the position of the other arm around their shared vertex! Notice that the size of an angle depends purely on the degree or fraction of turning, not on how long the drawn arms are. Even if you draw two short pencil lines or two kilometer-long laser beams, if the amount of turning between them is the same, the angle is identical. Straw strips fastened with a split pin provide the ideal tool to demonstrate angles of every conceivable opening.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Anatomy of an Angle', 'content': 'An angle consists of two straight arms (rays) meeting at a single common corner called the vertex. The measure of the angle is the amount of turning between these arms.', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Arm Length vs Angle Size', 'content': 'The size of an angle is determined solely by the amount of rotation between its arms. Extending or shortening the drawn arms does not alter the angle measure in any way.', 'icon': '📏'}, {'title': 'The Dynamic Ray Model', 'content': 'Think of one arm as a stationary baseline and the other arm as a rotating pointer. The gap swept open between them is the angle.', 'icon': '🧭'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the common meeting point of the two arms of an angle called?', 'options': ['Vertex', 'Circumference', 'Perimeter', 'Radius'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! The vertex is the shared endpoint where the two rays or arms meet.'}, {'question': 'If you lengthen the drawn lines of an angle without rotating them, what happens to the angle?', 'options': ['The angle remains exactly the same', 'The angle becomes twice as large', 'The angle shrinks into an acute angle', 'The angle turns into a straight line'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Angle size depends solely on the amount of turning between the arms, not on arm length.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'heading': 'Right Angle', 'tagline': 'The Benchmark Quarter Turn and Perfect L-Corner', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-green-700', 'badge': '1/4 Turn Benchmark', 'audio_text': "The most important benchmark in practical geometry is the right angle. A right angle is created by exactly a quarter turn, which is 1/4 of a full rotation. When two arms meet at a right angle, they form a crisp, perpendicular L-shape, like the corner of your textbook, the edge of a doorframe, or the tiles on your classroom floor. In angular units, a full turn contains 360 degrees, so one quarter of 360 degrees equals precisely 90 degrees. Whenever we see a 90-degree right angle in geometry drawings, we indicate it with a distinctive square corner symbol rather than a rounded arc. Look at the hands of a clock at 3 o'clock or 9 o'clock: the hour hand and minute hand are separated by exactly a quarter turn, forming a pristine right angle. Architects and builders rely on right angles to ensure that walls stand straight, floors remain level, and buildings stand upright securely.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '1/4 Turn = Right Angle', 'content': 'A quarter turn sweeps out exactly 1/4 of a circle (90°). The two arms are perpendicular to each other, forming an upright L-corner.', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Square Symbol Marking', 'content': 'Unlike other angles that use curved arcs, right angles are marked with a distinct square symbol at the vertex to signify exact perpendicularity.', 'icon': '⏹️'}, {'title': 'Everyday Right Angles', 'content': 'Textbook corners, mobile phone screens, windows, doors, chessboards, and clock hands at 3:00 and 9:00 all display perfect right angles.', 'icon': '🪟'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What fraction of a full turn forms a right angle?', 'options': ['1/4 turn (quarter turn)', '1/2 turn (half turn)', '3/4 turn (three-quarter turn)', '1/8 turn (eighth turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Excellent! A quarter turn (1/4 turn = 90°) creates a perfect right angle.'}, {'question': 'Which clock time displays a perfect right angle between the hour and minute hands?', 'options': ['3:00', '6:00', '12:00', '1:30'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! At 3:00, the minute hand points to 12 and the hour hand points to 3, forming a 1/4 turn (90°).'}]}, {'id': 5, 'heading': 'Acute Angle', 'tagline': 'Sharp Turns Less Than a Quarter Turn', 'color': 'from-cyan-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Less Than 1/4 Turn', 'audio_text': "Now let us examine angles that are smaller than a right angle. An acute angle is created by a turn that is less than a quarter turn. Because a quarter turn equals 90 degrees, an acute angle measures greater than 0 degrees but strictly less than 90 degrees. The word acute comes from a Latin word meaning sharp or pointed. Think of the sharp tip of a sharpened pencil, the partially opened blades of a pair of scissors, a slice of pizza cut into 8 pieces, or the beak of a sparrow picking seeds. When the hands of a clock show 1 o'clock or 2 o'clock, the opening between the hands is distinctly less than a quarter turn, making it an acute angle. Whenever you see an angle whose opening is narrow and sharp, narrower than the square corner of a sheet of paper, you are observing an acute angle!", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Definition of Acute Angle', 'content': 'An angle produced by a turn strictly less than 1/4 turn (between 0° and 90°). The opening is narrower than a right angle.', 'icon': '🔺'}, {'title': 'Visual Characteristics', 'content': "Acute angles appear sharp, pointed, and narrow. If you slide a right-angled paper corner into an acute angle, the angle's arm falls inside the paper.", 'icon': '✂️'}, {'title': 'Real-World Examples', 'content': 'Partially opened scissors, alligator jaws opening slightly, clock hands at 2:00 (60°), pencil tips, and slices of watermelon.', 'icon': '🐊'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How is an acute angle defined in terms of turns?', 'options': ['A turn less than a quarter (1/4) turn', 'A turn exactly equal to a quarter turn', 'A turn greater than a quarter turn but less than half turn', 'A turn equal to two quarter turns'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Any angle formed by a turn less than a quarter turn (< 90°) is acute.'}, {'question': 'At 2:00, what type of angle is formed between the clock hands?', 'options': ['Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! At 2:00, the hands span 2 clock numbers (60°), which is less than a 1/4 turn, forming an acute angle.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'heading': 'Obtuse Angle', 'tagline': 'Wide Turns Greater Than a Quarter Turn but Less Than a Half Turn', 'color': 'from-purple-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Between 1/4 and 1/2 Turn', 'audio_text': "Let us continue rotating past the right angle! When a turn is greater than a quarter turn, but less than a half turn, it produces an obtuse angle. In degrees, an obtuse angle measures strictly between 90 degrees and 180 degrees. The word obtuse means blunt or wide. An obtuse angle looks wide open, spread apart much broader than a standard square corner. Think of a reclining deck chair pushed back for sunbathing, a roof with a wide slope, a book opened wide flat across a lectern, or the blades of a windmill sweeping wide apart. Look at a clock dial at 4 o'clock or 5 o'clock: the separation between the hands is noticeably wider than a right angle, but has not yet formed a single straight line. Whenever an angle's arms spread out wider than the corner of a paper tester without lying in a straight line, it is an obtuse angle.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Definition of Obtuse Angle', 'content': 'An angle formed by a turn greater than 1/4 turn (90°) and strictly less than 1/2 turn (180°). It is open wider than a right angle.', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Visual Characteristics', 'content': 'Obtuse angles look broad, blunt, and sprawling. A square paper tester placed inside an obtuse angle will leave extra empty space visible.', 'icon': '🛋️'}, {'title': 'Everyday Obtuse Angles', 'content': 'Reclining beach chairs, open laptop screens pushed past 90°, clock hands at 4:00 (120°) and 5:00 (150°), and wide residential roof gables.', 'icon': '💻'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'An obtuse angle is created by a turn that is:', 'options': ['Greater than a 1/4 turn but less than a 1/2 turn', 'Strictly less than a 1/4 turn', 'Exactly equal to a 1/4 turn', 'Equal to one complete full turn'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! An obtuse angle spans between 1/4 turn (90°) and 1/2 turn (180°).'}, {'question': 'What type of angle do the clock hands form at 4:00?', 'options': ['Obtuse angle', 'Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! At 4:00, the hands span 4 clock numbers (120°), which is wider than 90° but less than 180°, making it obtuse.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'heading': 'Straight Angle', 'tagline': 'Two Quarter Turns Forming a Perfectly Flat Line', 'color': 'from-rose-600 to-red-700', 'badge': '1/2 Turn = 2 Quarter Turns', 'audio_text': "What happens if we rotate an arm through two full quarter turns in sequence? 1/4 turn plus another 1/4 turn equals 2/4, which simplifies to exactly 1/2 of a full turn. When an arm executes a half turn, it points in the direction exactly opposite to the starting arm. The two arms now form a single, unbroken straight line across the shared vertex! This special geometric configuration is called a straight angle. A straight angle measures exactly 180 degrees, which is the sum of two 90-degree right angles: 90 degrees plus 90 degrees equals 180 degrees. Observe a clock at exactly 6 o'clock: the hour hand points straight down to 6 while the minute hand points straight up to 12. Together, the hands form a vertical line passing through the centre, creating an unmistakable straight angle. A gymnast performing a full split, or an opened ruler spread flat on a table, perfectly demonstrates a straight angle.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Two Quarter Turns = Half Turn', 'content': 'Combining two consecutive 1/4 turns yields 1/2 turn (180°). The two arms lie in opposite directions along a single straight line.', 'icon': '📏'}, {'title': '180° Flat Angle', 'content': 'A straight angle equals two right angles combined (90° + 90° = 180°). The angle arc looks like a perfect semicircle.', 'icon': '🌗'}, {'title': 'Classic Clock Example', 'content': 'At 6:00, the minute hand pointing to 12 and the hour hand pointing to 6 form a straight line, showcasing a straight angle.', 'icon': '🕕'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A straight angle is created by what fraction of a full turn?', 'options': ['1/2 turn (two quarter turns)', '1/4 turn (one quarter turn)', '3/4 turn (three quarter turns)', '1 full turn (four quarter turns)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! A straight angle is formed by a 1/2 turn, which equals two 1/4 turns (180°).'}, {'question': 'Which clock time shows the hour hand and minute hand forming a straight angle?', 'options': ['6:00', '3:00', '9:00', '12:00'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! At 6:00, the hands point in opposite directions to form a straight 180° line.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'heading': 'Paper Fan', 'tagline': 'Folding and Unfolding Angles with Pleated Paper', 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-yellow-600', 'badge': 'Hands-On Activity 1', 'audio_text': 'Let us explore our first hands-on laboratory activity from the textbook: making a paper fan to visualize every type of angle! Take a rectangular sheet of craft paper and fold it back and forth into accordion pleats, about one centimetre wide. Pinch one end tightly with a rubber band or thread to create the pivot vertex, while leaving the other end free to open. When the fan is held almost closed, the opening between the two outer edges is narrow, forming a sharp acute angle. Open the fan wider until the outer ribs form an upright L-shape: you have just modeled a crisp right angle! Spread the fan wider still, so it spans past the square corner: now the ribs display an obtuse angle. If you pull the outer edges completely flat across from each other, the fan creates a semicircular straight angle. And if you glue two fans back-to-back and open them fully into a complete wheel, you demonstrate a full turn! Through this simple craft, every family of angles comes alive in your hands.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Accordion Paper Folding', 'content': 'Folding paper into uniform accordion pleats creates an adjustable geometric model that smoothly expands from 0° to 180° and beyond.', 'icon': '🪭'}, {'title': 'Dynamic Angle Progression', 'content': 'Closed = Acute angle; Quarter spread = Right angle; Wide spread = Obtuse angle; Flat spread = Straight angle; Full circle = Complete turn.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Pedagogical Value', 'content': 'Tactile manipulation helps internalize that angles are continuous spreads of turning rather than static disconnected drawings.', 'icon': '✨'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'When a folded paper fan is opened halfway into an upright L-shape, what angle does it represent?', 'options': ['Right angle', 'Acute angle', 'Straight angle', 'Full turn'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! An upright L-shape opening represents a 1/4 turn, or right angle.'}, {'question': 'What happens to the angle between the fan edges as the fan is opened wider from a right angle towards a straight line?', 'options': ['It becomes an obtuse angle', 'It becomes an acute angle', 'It shrinks to zero degrees', 'It turns into a square corner'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Opening wider than 1/4 turn but less than 1/2 turn forms an obtuse angle.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'heading': 'Angles Around', 'tagline': 'Discovering Angles in Daily Objects, Structures, and Sports', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-cyan-700', 'badge': 'Real-World Geometry', 'audio_text': 'Angles are not confined to geometry textbooks; they structure the entire world around us! Consider two hardcover books stood on end to form an A-frame tent roof: the peak where the covers touch creates an acute or obtuse angle depending on how far apart the bases are spread. Look at the door to your room: when shut, the angle between the door and wall is zero; when opened slightly, it forms an acute angle; when swung perpendicular to the doorway, it locks into a crisp right angle; and when pushed wide against the wall, it swings through an obtuse angle! Your laptop screen can tilt from an acute angle when half-closed, to an upright right angle, to a reclining obtuse angle for comfortable reading. In sports, gymnasts and yoga practitioners showcase angles dynamically: a gymnast performing a side split opens their legs into an impressive 180-degree straight angle, while a high hurdle jump demands an acute angle between the torso and thighs. By identifying angles in our surroundings, we recognize how architects, engineers, and athletes master geometry every day.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Hinges and Openings', 'content': 'Doors, windows, laptop screens, and book covers all pivot along a hinge vertex. Changing the opening width dynamically transitions the angle from acute to right to obtuse.', 'icon': '🚪'}, {'title': 'Architectural Roof Trusses', 'content': 'Steep alpine roofs form sharp acute angles to shed snow, while wide residential carports and warehouse roofs form obtuse angles to cover maximum ground span.', 'icon': '🏠'}, {'title': 'Athletic Angles in Gymnastics', 'content': 'Gymnastic floor routines, high kicks, and splits demonstrate human biomechanics as dynamic angle turns from acute tucks to straight 180° splits.', 'icon': '🤸'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'When a room door is opened halfway so that it stands perpendicular to the doorframe, what angle does it form?', 'options': ['Right angle', 'Acute angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! A door perpendicular to its frame forms a 1/4 turn, or right angle.'}, {'question': 'What angle is formed between the legs of a gymnast performing a complete flat floor split?', 'options': ['Straight angle (180°)', 'Right angle (90°)', 'Acute angle (< 90°)', 'Full turn (360°)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! In a full floor split, both legs stretch in opposite directions along a straight 180° line.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'heading': 'Folded Tester', 'tagline': 'Building an 8-Part Circular Angle Measuring Tool', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Laboratory Craft 1', 'audio_text': 'To measure angles accurately without an expensive protractor, we can construct our own precision angle-measuring tool using a circular sheet of tracing paper! Follow these simple steps from our textbook. First, cut out a neat circle of paper. Fold it exactly in half to create a semicircle: this fold line represents a 1/2 turn, or straight angle. Next, fold the semicircle in half once more: this creates a quarter-circle quadrant, representing a 1/4 turn, or right angle! Now fold this quadrant in half a third time: you divide the quarter turn into two equal halves. When you unfold the circle completely, the crease marks partition the whole circle into 8 identical wedges! Each individual wedge represents exactly 1/8 of a full turn. Because a full circle contains 360 degrees, each 1/8 turn equals 360 divided by 8, which is precisely 45 degrees. By placing this 8-part folded tester over any corner or shape, you can instantly test whether an angle is 1/8 turn, 1/4 turn, 3/8 turn, or a half turn!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '3-Fold Construction Process', 'content': 'Fold 1: Circle in half = 2 halves (1/2 turn). Fold 2: Semicircle in half = 4 quarters (1/4 turn). Fold 3: Quadrant in half = 8 equal wedges (1/8 turn).', 'icon': '✂️'}, {'title': 'The 1/8 Turn Unit', 'content': 'Each sector is 1/8 of a full turn (45°). Two 1/8 sectors combine to form a 1/4 turn right angle (2/8 = 1/4 = 90°).', 'icon': '🍕'}, {'title': 'Practical Field Testing', 'content': 'Lay the tester flat on scissors, corners, drawings, or pattern blocks. Count the sectors spanned between the arms to read the angle directly.', 'icon': '📐'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many equal sectors are created when a circular paper is folded in half three times?', 'options': ['8 equal sectors', '4 equal sectors', '6 equal sectors', '16 equal sectors'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Each fold doubles the sections: 2, then 4, then 8 equal sectors.'}, {'question': 'How many 1/8 sectors of the folded tester make up a right angle?', 'options': ['2 sectors (2/8 = 1/4)', '1 sector (1/8)', '3 sectors (3/8)', '4 sectors (4/8)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Two 1/8 sectors equal 2/8 = 1/4 of a turn, which is a right angle (45° + 45° = 90°).'}]}, {'id': 11, 'heading': 'Six-Part Tester', 'tagline': 'Folding 6 Equal Sectors with an Attached Straw Pointer', 'color': 'from-violet-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Laboratory Craft 2', 'audio_text': 'Our textbook introduces a second ingenious angle tool: the six-part tester equipped with an attached straw pointer! To build this tool, we divide a circular disc into 6 equal sectors rather than 8. Each sector of a six-part tester represents 1/6 of a full turn. Since a full turn is 360 degrees, 360 divided by 6 gives exactly 60 degrees for each sector! Notice that two of these sectors combine to make 2/6, which simplifies to 1/3 of a full turn, or 120 degrees: a prominent obtuse angle found in regular hexagons. Three sectors make 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2 of a full turn: a straight angle of 180 degrees. By pushing a split pin through the centre and attaching a plastic drinking straw, you create a rotating pointer. You can align the baseline of the disc with one arm of any drawn figure, and spin the straw pointer until it aligns with the second arm. The numbered or colored sectors underneath immediately reveal the exact turn fraction: 1/6 turn, 2/6 turn, 3/6 turn, or more!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The 1/6 Turn Sector', 'content': 'Dividing the circle into 6 equal parts creates 1/6 turn sectors (60° each). This matches the interior angles of equilateral triangles.', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Attached Straw Pointer', 'content': 'A split pin holds a movable straw pointer at the pivot. Turning the pointer lets students simulate dynamic angles and read sector counts.', 'icon': '🥤'}, {'title': 'Fraction Reductions', 'content': '1 sector = 1/6 turn (60°); 2 sectors = 2/6 = 1/3 turn (120°); 3 sectors = 3/6 = 1/2 turn (180°); 6 sectors = 6/6 = 1 full turn (360°).', 'icon': '🔢'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the angle measure of each sector in a circular tester divided into 6 equal parts?', 'options': ['1/6 of a turn (60°)', '1/4 of a turn (90°)', '1/8 of a turn (45°)', '1/2 of a turn (180°)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 360° divided by 6 equals 60°, which is 1/6 of a full turn.'}, {'question': 'How many sectors of the six-part tester combine to form a straight angle (1/2 turn)?', 'options': ['3 sectors (3/6 = 1/2)', '2 sectors (2/6 = 1/3)', '4 sectors (4/6 = 2/3)', '6 sectors (6/6 = 1)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 3 sectors make 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2 of a full turn (3 × 60° = 180°).'}]}, {'id': 12, 'heading': 'Combining Turns', 'tagline': 'Adding and Subtracting Fractional Turn Angles', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Fractional Turn Arithmetic', 'audio_text': 'Just as we add and subtract numbers, turns can be combined sequentially to create larger or smaller angles! Suppose you perform a quarter turn, which is 1/4 of a turn, and then immediately turn an additional 1/8 of a turn in the same direction. How much total turning have you executed? Let us convert 1/4 into eighths: 1/4 is equivalent to 2/8. Now add: 2/8 plus 1/8 equals 3/8 of a full turn! Because 3/8 is greater than 2/8 (which is 1/4) but less than 4/8 (which is 1/2), a 3/8 turn produces an obtuse angle of 135 degrees. What if you perform a half turn, which is 1/2 or 4/8, and then turn back by 1/8 turn in the opposite direction? 4/8 minus 1/8 leaves 3/8 turn remaining! Mastering the addition and subtraction of rotational fractions enables us to navigate complex courses, solve geometric riddles, and understand how compound gears operate in machinery.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Adding Fractional Turns', 'content': 'To add turns, find a common denominator. Example: 1/4 turn + 1/8 turn = 2/8 + 1/8 = 3/8 turn (135° obtuse angle).', 'icon': '➕'}, {'title': 'Subtracting Counter Turns', 'content': 'Turning in the opposite direction subtracts rotation. Example: 1/2 turn - 1/8 turn = 4/8 - 1/8 = 3/8 turn.', 'icon': '➖'}, {'title': 'Equivalence Benchmarks', 'content': '1/8 turn = 45°; 2/8 = 1/4 turn = 90°; 3/8 turn = 135°; 4/8 = 1/2 turn = 180°; 8/8 turn = 1 whole turn = 360°.', 'icon': '🧭'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the total turn when you combine a 1/4 turn and a 1/8 turn in the same direction?', 'options': ['3/8 turn', '2/8 turn', '1/2 turn', '5/8 turn'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 1/4 = 2/8, and 2/8 + 1/8 = 3/8 of a full turn (135°).'}, {'question': 'If you make a 1/2 turn and then turn backwards by 1/4 turn, what turn fraction remains?', 'options': ['1/4 turn', '1/8 turn', '3/8 turn', '1/2 turn'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 1/2 - 1/4 = 2/4 - 1/4 = 1/4 turn (a right angle).'}]}, {'id': 13, 'heading': 'Clock Turns', 'tagline': 'Tracking Minute-Hand Rotations Over 15, 30, 45, and 60 Minutes', 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-orange-700', 'badge': 'Time & Angle Rotations', 'audio_text': 'A standard analogue clock dial is divided into 12 equal hour marks, making it a wonderful real-world angle calculator! A full hour consists of 60 minutes, during which the minute hand completes one whole turn of 360 degrees. Therefore, each single hour number represents 360 divided by 12, which is 30 degrees of rotation. When the minute hand advances by 15 minutes, it moves from 12 to 3. 15 minutes out of 60 minutes is 15/60, which simplifies to 1/4 of a turn: a 90-degree right angle! When it travels for 30 minutes, moving from 12 to 6, 30/60 equals 1/2 of a turn: a 180-degree straight angle. When it advances for 45 minutes, from 12 to 9, 45/60 simplifies to 3/4 of a turn, or 270 degrees. And what about a short 5-minute interval? 5 minutes is 5/60, which simplifies to 1/12 of a turn, or exactly 30 degrees. Every passing minute is a precise geometrical turn in action!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '15 Minutes = 1/4 Turn', 'content': '15 out of 60 minutes = 15/60 = 1/4 turn (90° right angle). The minute hand travels from 12 to 3.', 'icon': '🕒'}, {'title': '30 Minutes = 1/2 Turn', 'content': '30 out of 60 minutes = 30/60 = 1/2 turn (180° straight angle). The minute hand travels from 12 to 6.', 'icon': '🕕'}, {'title': '45 Minutes = 3/4 Turn', 'content': '45 out of 60 minutes = 45/60 = 3/4 turn (270° reflex angle). The minute hand travels from 12 to 9.', 'icon': '🕘'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What fraction of a turn does the minute hand make as it moves from 12 to 3?', 'options': ['1/4 turn (15 minutes)', '1/2 turn (30 minutes)', '3/4 turn (45 minutes)', '1/12 turn (5 minutes)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Moving from 12 to 3 is 15 minutes, which is 15/60 = 1/4 turn (90°).'}, {'question': 'How many degrees does the minute hand turn in 30 minutes?', 'options': ['180° (1/2 turn)', '90° (1/4 turn)', '270° (3/4 turn)', '360° (1 full turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 30 minutes represents half the dial, which is 1/2 turn = 180°.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'heading': 'Clockwise Motion', 'tagline': 'Following the Traditional Forward Path of Clock Hands', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Directional Rotation', 'audio_text': "When talking about turns, describing the amount of turning is only half the story; we must also specify the direction of turning! Clockwise movement is defined as rotation in the same direction that the hands of a clock naturally move around the dial: from the top towards the right, then down to the bottom, and back up through the left. Think of tightening a screw into wood, or turning a water tap to close it tightly shut: both follow the clockwise path! In our homes and schools, the classic mnemonic rule is 'righty-tighty': rotating an object towards the right (clockwise) screws it in and seals it closed. When runners race around an outdoor athletics track in many international events, or when mechanical gears mesh to drive a vehicle forward, clockwise rotation directs the momentum. Whenever an arrow sweeps from 12 towards 1, 2, and 3, it is travelling clockwise.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Definition of Clockwise', 'content': 'Rotation that moves in the same direction as clock hands: Top → Right → Bottom → Left (12 → 3 → 6 → 9 → 12).', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Everyday Clockwise Actions', 'content': "Tightening a screw, screwing on a jar lid, turning off a water tap ('righty-tighty'), and winding a mechanical wristwatch.", 'icon': '🔩'}, {'title': 'Geometric Representation', 'content': 'Clockwise angles are drawn with circular arrows curving towards the right around the pivot point.', 'icon': '🏹'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the direction of movement that follows the natural path of clock hands called?', 'options': ['Clockwise', 'Anti-clockwise', 'Linear', 'Perpendicular'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Movement following the path of clock hands is termed clockwise.'}, {'question': 'Which household action typically requires a clockwise turn?', 'options': ['Tightening a water tap to stop water flow', 'Opening a sealed jam jar lid', 'Loosening a tight bolt', 'Unwinding a garden hose reel in reverse'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Turning a tap clockwise tightens the internal valve and stops the water flow.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'heading': 'Anti-Clockwise', 'tagline': 'Reversing the Turn: Opening Taps, Lids, and Counter-Rotations', 'color': 'from-rose-600 to-pink-700', 'badge': 'Counter Rotation', 'audio_text': "Now let us examine the opposite rotational path: anti-clockwise movement! Anti-clockwise rotation, also known in some regions as counter-clockwise, is movement in the direction opposite to that of clock hands: from the top towards the left, then down to the bottom, and back up through the right. Think of opening a tight water tap to let cool water pour out: you turn the handle anti-clockwise! When you unscrew the cap of a water bottle or twist open a jar of delicious mango pickle, your hand turns anti-clockwise to release the lid. The popular memory phrase is 'lefty-loosey': turning towards the left (anti-clockwise) loosens threads and opens seals. In mathematics and trigonometry, positive angles are traditionally measured in the anti-clockwise direction starting from the positive horizontal axis. Understanding both clockwise and anti-clockwise turns gives you complete navigational control over any rotational puzzle.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Definition of Anti-Clockwise', 'content': 'Rotation in the direction opposite to clock hands: Top → Left → Bottom → Right (12 → 9 → 6 → 3 → 12).', 'icon': '🔁'}, {'title': 'Everyday Anti-Clockwise Actions', 'content': "Unscrewing bottle caps, loosening bolts, opening water taps ('lefty-loosey'), and spinning revolving doors in reverse.", 'icon': '🚰'}, {'title': 'Mathematical Convention', 'content': 'In higher mathematics, the anti-clockwise direction is chosen as the standard positive rotational direction.', 'icon': '📐'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In which direction do you turn a bottle cap to open and unscrew it?', 'options': ["Anti-clockwise ('lefty-loosey')", "Clockwise ('righty-tighty')", 'Straight downward without turning', 'Side-to-side horizontally'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': "Correct! Unscrewing a bottle cap requires an anti-clockwise turn ('lefty-loosey')."}, {'question': 'If an arrow at 12 on a clock turns anti-clockwise by a 1/4 turn, which number does it point to?', 'options': ['9', '3', '6', '12'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Turning anti-clockwise from 12 by 1/4 turn sweeps to the left, pointing to 9.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'heading': 'Compass Turns', 'tagline': 'Navigating Cardinal Directions with Clockwise and Anti-Clockwise Turns', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Cardinal Navigation Game', 'audio_text': 'Let us put all our knowledge together with the classic textbook directional game table! Imagine standing at the centre of a large compass rose with four cardinal directions: North pointing forward, South pointing straight behind you, East pointing to your right, and West pointing to your left. Between any two adjacent cardinal directions, such as North and East, there is exactly one quarter turn, or 90 degrees. If you are facing North and make a 1/4 turn clockwise, you end up facing East! If you are facing North and make a 1/4 turn anti-clockwise, you end up facing West. What if you face East and make a 1/2 turn? A 1/2 turn always reverses your heading, so you end up facing West! What if you start facing South and make a 3/4 turn clockwise? One quarter turn takes you to West, two takes you to North, and three brings you to East! By mapping turn fractions and directions onto cardinal compass points, you master both spatial orientation and geometric angle rotation.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The 4 Cardinal Directions', 'content': 'North (N), East (E), South (S), West (W). Each adjacent pair is separated by a 1/4 turn (90° right angle).', 'icon': '🧭'}, {'title': 'Clockwise Compass Sequence', 'content': 'N → (1/4 turn CW) → E → (1/4 turn CW) → S → (1/4 turn CW) → W → (1/4 turn CW) → N.', 'icon': '➡️'}, {'title': 'Anti-Clockwise Compass Sequence', 'content': 'N → (1/4 turn ACW) → W → (1/4 turn ACW) → S → (1/4 turn ACW) → E → (1/4 turn ACW) → N.', 'icon': '⬅️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If you are facing North and make a 1/4 turn clockwise, which direction do you face?', 'options': ['East', 'West', 'South', 'North'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Turning 1/4 turn clockwise from North brings you directly to East.'}, {'question': 'Starting at West, you make a 1/2 turn. Which direction do you face?', 'options': ['East', 'North', 'South', 'West'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! A 1/2 turn always points in the exact opposite direction: opposite of West is East.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'heading': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Turns, Rays, Vertices, and Angle Classifications', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 1', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 1! Test your foundational mastery of turns, rays, vertices, and angle classifications. Examine each question carefully, choose the correct answer, and review the detailed step-by-step solution modal for complete mathematical clarity.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is an angle formed by two rays meeting at a common point called?', 'options': ['A Vertex and its Arms', 'A Linear Segment', 'A Perimeter', 'A Circumference'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'An angle is formed when two rays (arms) share a common endpoint called the vertex. The opening between the arms measures the amount of turning.'}, {'question': 'How many degrees are in one complete full turn?', 'options': ['360°', '180°', '90°', '270°'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'One complete full turn around a circular pivot sweeps out 360°.'}, {'question': 'A right angle is created by which fraction of a full turn?', 'options': ['1/4 turn', '1/2 turn', '1/8 turn', '3/4 turn'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A right angle is formed by exactly a quarter turn (1/4 turn = 360° / 4 = 90°).'}, {'question': 'Which angle has an opening strictly less than a quarter turn (between 0° and 90°)?', 'options': ['Acute angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Right angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'An acute angle is defined as any angle whose turn is strictly less than 1/4 turn (< 90°).'}, {'question': 'An angle with an opening greater than 1/4 turn but less than 1/2 turn is classified as:', 'options': ['Obtuse angle', 'Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'An obtuse angle lies strictly between 1/4 turn (90°) and 1/2 turn (180°).'}, {'question': 'A straight angle is created by two quarter turns. What is its measure in degrees?', 'options': ['180°', '90°', '360°', '270°'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Two quarter turns equal 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 turn = 90° + 90° = 180°, forming a straight angle.'}, {'question': 'If you double the length of the lines drawn for an angle, how does the angle measure change?', 'options': ['It remains unchanged', 'It doubles in size', 'It is halved', 'It becomes 90°'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'The size of an angle depends purely on the degree of turning between its arms, not the physical length of the drawn line segments.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following geometric corners is a classic right angle?', 'options': ['The corner of a rectangular postcard', 'The sharp tip of a sewing needle', 'An open book lying almost flat', 'A slice of 8-piece pizza'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Every corner of a rectangular postcard forms an exact perpendicular L-shape (90° right angle).'}, {'question': 'How many right angles combine to make a straight angle?', 'options': ['2 right angles', '1 right angle', '3 right angles', '4 right angles'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Two 90° right angles combine to make 90° + 90° = 180°, which is a straight angle.'}, {'question': 'How many right angles are needed to make one complete full turn?', 'options': ['4 right angles', '2 right angles', '3 right angles', '8 right angles'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Four quarter turns (4 × 90° = 360°) complete one whole circular rotation.'}]}, {'id': 18, 'heading': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Paper Tools, Real-World Hinges, and Sector Testers', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 2', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 2! Test your practical understanding of paper folding testers, sector calculations, and real-world angle structures. Work through each scenario systematically and click on any question to inspect its complete solution.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'In an 8-part folded circular angle tester, what is the angle measure of each wedge?', 'options': ['45° (1/8 turn)', '60° (1/6 turn)', '90° (1/4 turn)', '30° (1/12 turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A full circle has 360°. Folding into 8 equal parts gives 360° / 8 = 45° (1/8 of a turn) per wedge.'}, {'question': 'How many 1/8 sectors of an 8-part tester are required to match an obtuse angle of 135°?', 'options': ['3 sectors (3 × 45° = 135°)', '2 sectors (90°)', '4 sectors (180°)', '1 sector (45°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Each sector is 45°. 3 sectors × 45° = 135°, which is an obtuse angle (3/8 turn).'}, {'question': 'In a 6-part tester with a straw pointer, what is the angle size of each sector?', 'options': ['60° (1/6 turn)', '45° (1/8 turn)', '90° (1/4 turn)', '120° (1/3 turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Dividing 360° into 6 equal parts gives 360° / 6 = 60° (1/6 of a full turn) per sector.'}, {'question': 'In a regular hexagon, each interior angle measures 120°. How many 1/6 sectors is this?', 'options': ['2 sectors (2 × 60° = 120°)', '1 sector (60°)', '3 sectors (180°)', '4 sectors (240°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Each sector in a 6-part tester is 60°. 120° / 60° = 2 sectors (2/6 = 1/3 of a full turn).'}, {'question': 'When an accordion paper fan is opened completely flat across the base, what angle is formed?', 'options': ['Straight angle (180°)', 'Right angle (90°)', 'Acute angle (< 90°)', 'Full turn (360°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Opening the fan flat across both opposite sides spreads it into a semicircle, forming a 180° straight angle.'}, {'question': 'What angle is formed between two covers of an open laptop tilted back at 110°?', 'options': ['Obtuse angle', 'Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '110° is greater than 90° and less than 180°, so it is classified as an obtuse angle.'}, {'question': 'A pair of scissors is opened slightly so the blades form a 35° opening. What type of angle is this?', 'options': ['Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Straight angle'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '35° is strictly less than 90°, making it a sharp acute angle.'}, {'question': 'If an angle tester made of two cardboard strips pinned at the centre is set to a right angle, which shape fits snugly into it?', 'options': ['The square corner of a book cover', 'A sharp pencil point', 'A wide reclining beach chair', 'A circular coin edge'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'The square corner of a book cover is exactly 90°, so it fits perfectly against a right-angle tester.'}, {'question': 'How many folds in half of a circular paper are needed to produce an 8-part tester?', 'options': ['3 folds (2 → 4 → 8)', '2 folds (2 → 4)', '4 folds (2 → 4 → 8 → 16)', '1 fold (2)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Fold 1 gives 2 parts; fold 2 gives 4 parts; fold 3 gives 8 equal parts.'}, {'question': 'In an equilateral triangle, all three angles are equal to 60°. What type of angles are they?', 'options': ['Acute angles', 'Right angles', 'Obtuse angles', 'Straight angles'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Because 60° is less than 90°, all three angles of an equilateral triangle are acute angles.'}]}, {'id': 19, 'heading': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Fraction Turn Arithmetic and Clock Dial Angles', 'color': 'from-cyan-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 3', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 3! Dive into turn arithmetic, fractional additions, and clock-face angles. Calculate each rotational fraction methodically, submit your answer, and verify your logic with the step-by-step solution modal.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is the result of combining a 1/4 turn and a 1/8 turn in the same direction?', 'options': ['3/8 turn (135°)', '2/8 turn (90°)', '1/2 turn (180°)', '5/8 turn (225°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1/4 turn = 2/8 turn. Adding 2/8 + 1/8 = 3/8 turn. In degrees: 90° + 45° = 135° (obtuse angle).'}, {'question': 'If you turn 1/2 of a turn and then turn back 1/8 of a turn, what fraction of a turn remains?', 'options': ['3/8 turn', '1/4 turn', '1/8 turn', '5/8 turn'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1/2 turn = 4/8 turn. Subtracting the reverse turn: 4/8 - 1/8 = 3/8 turn.'}, {'question': 'What is the angle between the hour and minute hands of a clock at 3:00?', 'options': ['90° (Right angle)', '60° (Acute angle)', '120° (Obtuse angle)', '180° (Straight angle)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'At 3:00, the minute hand is at 12 and the hour hand is at 3, spanning 3 hours: 3 × 30° = 90° (a right angle).'}, {'question': 'What angle is formed between the hands of a clock at 6:00?', 'options': ['180° (Straight angle)', '90° (Right angle)', '120° (Obtuse angle)', '360° (Full turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'At 6:00, the hands point directly in opposite directions (12 and 6), forming a 180° straight angle.'}, {'question': 'How many degrees does the minute hand turn in 15 minutes?', 'options': ['90° (1/4 turn)', '180° (1/2 turn)', '45° (1/8 turn)', '60° (1/6 turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '15 minutes out of 60 minutes is 15/60 = 1/4 of a full turn: 1/4 × 360° = 90°.'}, {'question': 'How many degrees does the minute hand turn in 5 minutes?', 'options': ['30° (1/12 turn)', '15°', '45°', '60°'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '5 minutes out of 60 is 5/60 = 1/12 turn. 360° / 12 = 30°.'}, {'question': 'At 5:00, what type of angle is formed between the clock hands?', 'options': ['Obtuse angle (150°)', 'Acute angle (60°)', 'Right angle (90°)', 'Straight angle (180°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'At 5:00, the hands span 5 hour spaces: 5 × 30° = 150°. Since 150° is between 90° and 180°, it is an obtuse angle.'}, {'question': 'What fraction of a full turn does the minute hand complete in 45 minutes?', 'options': ['3/4 turn (270°)', '1/2 turn (180°)', '1/4 turn (90°)', '2/3 turn (240°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '45 out of 60 minutes = 45/60 = 3/4 turn (270°).'}, {'question': 'What is 1/4 turn + 1/4 turn + 1/4 turn equal to?', 'options': ['3/4 turn (270°)', '1/2 turn (180°)', '1 whole turn (360°)', '5/8 turn (225°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Adding three quarter turns gives 3 × (1/4) = 3/4 turn = 270°.'}, {'question': 'At 1:00, what type of angle is formed between the clock hands?', 'options': ['Acute angle (30°)', 'Right angle (90°)', 'Obtuse angle (120°)', 'Straight angle (180°)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'At 1:00, the hands span 1 hour mark, which is 30°. Since 30° is less than 90°, it is an acute angle.'}]}, {'id': 20, 'heading': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Directional Navigation, Clockwise & Anti-Clockwise Rotations', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 4', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to the final challenge! Test your advanced mastery of clockwise and anti-clockwise navigation across cardinal compass headings. Solve each directional puzzle, check your score, and celebrate your mastery of Angles as Turns!', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'Which direction of turning follows the natural path of clock hands?', 'options': ['Clockwise', 'Anti-clockwise', 'Straight line', 'Perpendicular'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Rotation that moves in the direction of clock hands (12 → 3 → 6 → 9) is called clockwise.'}, {'question': 'In which direction do you turn a standard screw to tighten it into wood?', 'options': ["Clockwise ('righty-tighty')", "Anti-clockwise ('lefty-loosey')", 'Upward vertically', 'Downward linearly'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': "Standard screw threads tighten when turned clockwise ('righty-tighty')."}, {'question': 'In which direction do you twist a jar lid to unscrew and loosen it?', 'options': ["Anti-clockwise ('lefty-loosey')", "Clockwise ('righty-tighty')", 'Straight upward without twisting', 'Clockwise then push down'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': "Twisting anti-clockwise loosens the lid threads ('lefty-loosey')."}, {'question': 'You are facing North. You make a 1/4 turn clockwise. Which direction do you now face?', 'options': ['East', 'West', 'South', 'North'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Starting at North, a 1/4 turn clockwise (90° right) leads directly to East.'}, {'question': 'You are facing North. You make a 1/4 turn anti-clockwise. Which direction do you now face?', 'options': ['West', 'East', 'South', 'North'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Starting at North, a 1/4 turn anti-clockwise (90° left) leads directly to West.'}, {'question': 'You are facing East. You execute a 1/2 turn. Which direction do you face?', 'options': ['West', 'North', 'South', 'East'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A 1/2 turn (180°) points to the exact opposite direction: opposite of East is West.'}, {'question': 'You are facing South. You make a 1/4 turn clockwise. Which direction do you face?', 'options': ['West', 'East', 'North', 'South'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': "From South, turning 1/4 turn clockwise (90° towards the clock's 9 position) leads to West."}, {'question': 'You are facing West. You make a 3/4 turn clockwise. Which direction do you face?', 'options': ['South', 'North', 'East', 'West'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'From West: 1/4 turn CW = North; 2/4 turn CW = East; 3/4 turn CW = South.'}, {'question': 'Starting at North, how many quarter turns clockwise are needed to face West?', 'options': ['3 quarter turns (3/4 turn)', '1 quarter turn (1/4 turn)', '2 quarter turns (1/2 turn)', '4 quarter turns (1 full turn)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'From North: 1 quarter turn CW = East, 2 = South, 3 = West. Thus, 3 quarter turns (3/4 turn CW).'}, {'question': 'If you make a 1/4 turn clockwise and then immediately make a 1/4 turn anti-clockwise, what is your net turn?', 'options': ['0 turns (Back to original facing direction)', '1/2 turn', '1/4 turn', '1 full turn'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A clockwise turn followed by an equal anti-clockwise turn cancels out: +1/4 - 1/4 = 0 net turn, returning you to the starting heading.'}]}]
